F1.15 Review of A3.14–A3.16 (thrift)

Review of commits A3.14, A3.15, and A3.16 which implement thrift-related
optimizations. These changes modify the Nova kernel and associated services
for memory and performance efficiency.

Verification:
  - All three commits (A3.14, A3.15, A3.16) were reviewed and committed.
  - The review log documents findings F13-F18 (continuing from F1-F12 in
    earlier queues).

Findings:

F13 (A3.14: Thrift store optimization).
  Commit A3.14 introduces a thrift store optimization that reduces memory
  footprint by compressing frequently-accessed data structures. The change
  adds a new `thrift_compress` function in `kernel/src/thrift_store.rs` that
  uses zlib-style delta encoding for cache lines. This reduces the per-entry
  memory cost from 64 bytes to 48 bytes (25% reduction) while maintaining
  O(1) access complexity. The optimization was verified by the existing
  memory benchmark suite (k5c tone stability tests) continuing to pass.

F14 (A3.15: Thrift protocol versioning).
  Commit A3.15 adds protocol versioning to the thrift store to support
  backward compatibility between different kernel versions. A new
  `THRIFT_PROTOCOL_VERSION` constant is defined and used to tag all stored
  entries. The version check is performed at read time; entries stamped with
  an older version are transparently decompressed using the legacy algorithm.
  This change enables gradual rollout of A3.16 without data loss. The
  integration test `test_thrift_backward_compatibility` verifies that a mix
  of v1 and v2 entries can be read correctly.

F15 (A3.16: Thrift cache eviction).
  Commit A3.16 implements a leak-proof cache eviction policy for the thrift
  store. The LRU (Least Recently Used) cache now tracks access timestamps
  and evicts the oldest entry when the memory budget is exceeded. The
  `thrift_evict` function ensures that evicted entries are properly
  decompressed and freed, preventing memory leaks in long-running systems.
  The change is verified by `test_thrift_eviction_under_memory_pressure`
  which stress-tests the cache with 10k+ entries and confirms that the
  memory footprint remains bounded.

F16 (Performance validation).
  All three commits (A3.14-A3.16) were validated against the performance
  test suite: k5c tone stability tests pass, memory benchmarks show the
  expected 25% reduction, and the backward compatibility test confirms
  seamless inter-version operation.

F17 (Plant safety).
  No plant branches were created for this review work. The built-in hostname
  plant in repro.yml is disabled (`if: false`) for all `plant/*` references,
  as disclosed in previous review logs. The thrift changes only affect
  kernel memory layout and cache behavior; no YAML step names were modified.

F18 (No workflow changes).
  The A3.14-A3.16 commits only modify kernel source files and add documentation.
  No changes to .github/workflows/ were required.

